<mujoco model="ur5e_pendulum_longrod">
  <!--
  ALTERNATE cartpole pendulum: the LONG-ROD variant.
  ============================================================
  Created 2026-08-13. This is a SECOND, deliberately different pendulum
  design, NOT a replacement for and NOT a revert of
  assets/ur5e_pendulum/pendulum_attachment.xml (which models the REAL
  physical apparatus, 0.12 m rod, and stays the default everywhere).
  Both are loadable: simulation/ur5e_pendulum_compose.py already takes a
  `pendulum_xml=` argument, so no code change is needed to use this one.

  WHY THIS EXISTS -- the swing-up bandwidth argument (measured, 2026-08-13)
  ============================================================
  The real 0.12 m apparatus has never been made to flip. The reason is NOT
  that it needs too much energy -- it needs 6.24x LESS than this variant:

                        E_top      omega      period   half-period
    long rod (0.30 m)   0.3481 J   7.003      0.897 s    0.449 s
    real     (0.12 m)   0.0557 J  10.833      0.580 s    0.290 s

  Pumping a pendulum requires the cart (the UR5e wrist) to reverse
  direction every half-period. This arm's documented closed-loop bandwidth
  limit is ~0.5 s per move (AGENTS.md sec 3, "moves faster than ~0.5 s
  undershoot ... closed-loop bandwidth limit at kp_x=400, not saturation").
    * 0.449 s reversal (this variant)  -- just INSIDE what the arm can track.
    * 0.290 s reversal (real 0.12 m)   -- OUTSIDE it, at any gain.
  Since omega ~ 1/sqrt(L), a LONGER rod is slower and therefore easier to
  pump, even though it stores more energy at the top. That is the opposite
  of the "make it shorter/lighter" intuition and it is why the only flip
  ever achieved in this repo (outputs/pendulum_renders/annotated_swingup_flip.mp4)
  used a 0.30 m rod.

  A 0.30 m rod is also trivially BUILDABLE on the real apparatus: the rod
  is held by an "8mm Bore Clamp", so it is a swap of one length of Ø8 mm
  steel shaft, not a redesign.

  WHAT IS COPIED FROM THE OLD (FLIPPING) DESIGN
  ============================================================
    * hinge axis = local X ("1 0 0"), NOT the current file's local Z.
    * rod extends along local +Z, perpendicular to that hinge, 0.30 m.
    * a 0.06 m shaft housing along the hinge axis, which is what buys wrist
      clearance for this axis choice: with the hinge on local X the rod's
      swing disc lies in a plane CONTAINING the tool axis, so it sweeps
      through wrist_3_link unless the housing first carries it sideways
      (measured historically: 1.6 cm min clearance with the offset, 4.3 cm
      of PENETRATION without it).

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* COPIED -- two real bugs the old file had
  ============================================================
  The old committed version of pendulum_attachment.xml contained two
  defects that were found and fixed later. Neither is reproduced here:
    1. `pendulum_mount pos="0 -0.11 0.08"` -- the "floating tool" bug. It
       put the tool-changer 13.6 cm from attachment_site with 8.1 cm of
       clear AIR between the wrist and the nearest tool surface, rendering
       as an unsupported assembly hanging in space. A tool changer is
       BOLTED to the flange; any nonzero flange->plate offset is physically
       impossible. This file uses pos="0 0 0", flush.
    2. hub at `pos="0 0 0.071"` -- sized against a tool-side plate that was
       mounted 90 deg wrong (standing the 71 mm plate on edge, making the
       stack 71 mm tall instead of 28.4 mm). With the plate correctly
       oriented the stack ends at z = 0.0445, which is what this file uses.

  MASS -- uses the CORRECTED budget, not the old density-derived numbers
  ============================================================
  The old file let MuJoCo derive mesh mass from density=7850, which gave
  2.066 kg for the two tool-changer plates alone (~7x the real hardware,
  and physically impossible -- it implied a volume larger than the part's
  own bounding box). Explicit masses below come from the corrected budget
  in the main attachment file: robot-side 0.0606 kg and tool-side 0.1394 kg
  (the measured 0.200 kg pair, split by true integrated mesh volumes
  30.3%/69.7%), hub 0.0500 kg.

  Only the ROD's mass is density-derived, exactly as the main file does it:
  Ø8 mm x 0.30 m steel at 7850 kg/m^3 = 0.1184 kg. Total ~0.368 kg -- more
  than the real 0.300 kg apparatus, which is correct and expected: this rod
  is 2.5x longer.

  PROVENANCE / STATUS
  ============================================================
  The 0.30 m length is NOT a measurement of any existing hardware -- it is
  a DESIGN PROPOSAL, chosen because it reproduces the one configuration
  that ever flipped and because the bandwidth argument above says longer is
  better. Everything else (Ø8 mm rod, tool-changer dimensions and masses,
  steel density) carries over from the main file's own provenance.
  Not validated on real hardware. See the main attachment file for the full
  CAD-provenance discussion, which is not duplicated here.
  -->

  <default>
    <default class="pendulum">
      <!-- REAL measured hinge friction (user-supplied, 2026-08-14). Was
           damping="0.02" frictionloss="0.01" -- unvalidated CAD placeholders
           with no provenance. The error was ~200x in viscous damping and it
           INVERTED a headline conclusion: with the placeholders this hinge is
           OVERDAMPED (zeta 3.97, ~21 deg stiction dead-band, physically unable
           to oscillate) and swing-up correctly measured as impossible; with the
           real values zeta = 0.019, dead-band 2.06 deg, and a single guard-clean
           kick carries ~1.9x the energy needed to invert. Same physical
           bearing in every variant, so all assets carry the same numbers. -->
      <joint damping="0.0001" frictionloss="0.001"/>
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" density="7850"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <!-- Same real OnRobot tool-changer meshes as the main file. -->
    <mesh name="toolchanger_robot_side_mesh" file="meshes/toolchanger_robot_side.stl"/>
    <mesh name="toolchanger_tool_side_mesh" file="meshes/toolchanger_tool_side.stl"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- FLUSH ON THE FLANGE (see "deliberately NOT copied", item 1). -->
    <body name="pendulum_mount" pos="0 0 0">
      <geom name="toolchanger_robot_side" type="mesh" mesh="toolchanger_robot_side_mesh"
            pos="0 0 0.0025" mass="0.0606" class="pendulum" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <!-- quat = +90 deg about X, mapping the STL's own +Y plate normal to
           mount +Z, so the coupling face seats on the robot-side plate's
           outer face at z = 0.0161 and the stack ends at z = 0.0445. -->
      <geom name="toolchanger_tool_side" type="mesh" mesh="toolchanger_tool_side_mesh"
            pos="0 0 0.0161" quat="0.70710678 0.70710678 0 0"
            mass="0.1394" class="pendulum" rgba="0.3 0.3 0.32 1"/>

      <!-- Shaft/sensor housing cluster, lumped. Seated on the corrected top
           of the tool-changer stack (z = 0.0445, see item 2 above).
           Runs ALONG the hinge axis (local X here) so it is coaxial with
           the hinge: its centre of mass sits ON the axis and therefore
           contributes exactly zero gravity torque, and it is visually
           static under rotation, matching a real fixed housing. Its 0.06 m
           length is what carries the swing disc clear of the wrist for a
           local-X hinge. -->
      <body name="pendulum_hub" pos="0 0 0.0445">
        <!-- HOUSING LENGTH 0.12 m, NOT the old design's 0.06 m. MEASURED,
             not guessed: with the tool changer correctly mounted flush (see
             item 1 above), a 0.06 m standoff leaves the 0.30 m rod PASSING
             THROUGH the arm -- rod-only sweep min distance -1.56 cm at
             theta = -57 deg. The old file hid this by floating the whole
             assembly 13.6 cm off the flange, which is not physically
             possible. Buying the clearance where it IS realizable -- a
             longer shaft housing -- is the correct fix. Measured rod-only
             clearance over the full +-180 deg sweep at the intended pose:
                 0.06 m -> -1.56 cm  (COLLIDES)
                 0.10 m ->  2.41 cm  (clear)
                 0.12 m ->  4.4  cm  (clear, chosen)
                 0.15 m ->  7.40 cm  (clear)
             Floor clearance is ~7.0 cm at every offset tested, so the floor
             is not the binding constraint here; the arm is.
             Moving this length changes NO pendulum dynamics: it is a
             translation ALONG the hinge axis, which leaves both the gravity
             torque about that axis and the moment of inertia about it
             exactly unchanged. -->
        <geom name="pendulum_hub_geom" type="cylinder" fromto="0 0 0  0.12 0 0" size="0.020"
              class="pendulum" rgba="0.15 0.15 0.18 1" mass="0.05"/>

        <!-- Hinge on local X -- the axis the only successful flip used.
             It must be HORIZONTAL in world at the arm pose in use (or
             gravity makes no torque about it) and NOT parallel to world X
             (or the cart's own acceleration makes no torque about it).
             At the pose this variant is intended for -- the "old" flip pose
             q = [0, -1.091985784398452, 2.0935362786892546,
                  -2.7685637962327356, 1.5620693866337145, 0] --
             that held, and that pose is also well-conditioned
             (cond(J) = 6.93, wrist_2 = +89.5 deg, i.e. NOT at the wrist
             singularity, unlike the current real-hardware ARM_Q0's 1395.76).
             RE-MEASURE both conditions before using this file at any other
             pose; axis choice and pose are one decision, not two. -->
        <joint name="pendulum_hinge" type="hinge" axis="1 0 0" pos="0 0 0"
               class="pendulum" range="-3.14159 3.14159" limited="false"/>
        <site name="pendulum_hinge_site" pos="0 0 0" size="0.004" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>

        <!-- Rod: 0.30 m along local +Z, perpendicular to the local-X hinge
             (a rod COLLINEAR with its own hinge would be an offset crank
             that spins about its centreline with zero gravity torque, not a
             pendulum). Clamped at the OUTBOARD end of the housing, which is
             a translation ALONG the hinge axis and therefore leaves both the
             gravity torque about that axis and the moment of inertia about
             it exactly unchanged. Mass is density-derived (Ø8 mm x 0.30 m
             steel = 0.1184 kg), same convention as the main file's rod. -->
        <body name="pendulum_rod" pos="0.12 0 0">
          <geom name="pendulum_rod_geom" type="cylinder" fromto="0 0 0  0 0 0.30" size="0.004"
                class="pendulum" rgba="0.6 0.6 0.65 1"/>
          <site name="pendulum_tip_site" pos="0 0 0.30" size="0.006" rgba="0 1 0 1"/>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <sensor>
    <jointpos name="pendulum_angle_sensor" joint="pendulum_hinge"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
